1. `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-settings-general@0.1.0-rc.5` and `-models@0.1.0-rc.5` are **NOT published on npm** (404). Only rc.6+ exist. So the plugin's expected artifacts (0.1.0-rc.5 with those hashes) **cannot be obtained from npm at all**.

2. rc.6 exists with different hashes (ffd5b564… / 203a4529…) — not matching the plugin's expectations either.

3. `dsh-web-app@0.1.0-rc.5` is also 404; rc.6 depends on settings owners at rc.6.

So: the plugin's compat check requires `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-settings-general@0.1.0-rc.5` + `...-models@0.1.0-rc.5` with exact client.js hashes — a version combination that isn't published on the public npm registry. Note the plugin's README says these are "exact-47f derivatives of the official Settings General and Models runtime owners" — i.e., the plugin *bundles its own copies* of these owners derived from commit 47f9438. The check `assertSupportedCompatOwners` resolves the *installed host's* versions from the anchor and compares against hashes the author computed from... hmm, expected `0.1.0-rc.5` version string. Since rc.5 isn't on npm, maybe those packages were published under a different registry or the version 0.1.0-rc.5 existed and was unpublished. Either way, on this machine (and from public npm today) **the exact required artifacts are unobtainable**.

Wait — maybe the check resolves the *anchor* = `join(dshHome(), "profiles", "auth-in-one-resolution-anchor.cjs")`, a file that the *bootstrap* would have created pointing at the running host's packages. In the desktop app's case, the anchor resolution would find the desktop's bundled packages (0.2.0-rc.2) → mismatch. And for the npm install, also 0.2.0-rc.2.

Also interesting: maybe the plugin author computed those hashes from the *desktop app build* of a specific DSH desktop version (not npm). The desktop app at some version may bundle settings owners labeled 0.1.0-rc.5. Our desktop is 0.2.0-rc.2.

